ABSTRACT = r"""When can a movement in financial conditions be read as a movement in monetary policy? Two independent calibrations answer. Announcement windows give weights on the 10-year yield, equity valuation and the dollar from their responses to policy news. The FRB/US model gives weights from their effects on GDP. For the mix of asset prices that follows a policy surprise the two readings coincide: by normalization for the market index, and for FRB/US at \bl{ag:h4:bpsi} (interval \bl{ag:h4:bpsi:lo} to \bl{ag:h4:bpsi:hi}) when movements are assumed to persist, \bl{ag:bp:hl4} with a half-life of four quarters. For other mixes they differ. The model gives the dollar \bl{ag:h4:rr} times the relative effect that the event study does, whatever persistence is assumed. The difference between the readings is about a third of the market move at every horizon from a day to a year, on announcement days and other days alike. It changes the reading of history when it comes from the equity weights, as in 1998--2001, when the full FRB/US index read \bl{br:ep1998:gap} points tighter, and not in 2022--24, when the two weightings agreed on the year's tightening and both overpredicted the rise in unemployment. Realized activity cannot tell the weightings apart. In the main sample the data reject neither the claim that the market reading is sufficient nor the claim that the model's is."""

INTRO = r"""\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
Consider two tightenings of financial conditions with the long yield unchanged. In one, equity valuations fall by 5 log points. In the other, the dollar appreciates by 3.9 log points. An index weighted by how asset prices respond to monetary policy news rates the two alike: each is worth about \bl{ag:ex:eq:mkt} points of the two-year yield. The FRB/US model, which weights asset prices by their effects on spending, rates the dollar movement \bl{ag:h4:fr} times as high as the equity movement (Table~\ref{tab:example}). When can a movement in financial conditions be read as a movement in monetary policy, and when does it matter that two readings differ?

Policymakers read the stance of policy from financial conditions. In November 2023 the Chair noted that ``Financial conditions have tightened significantly in recent months, driven by higher longer-term bond yields, among other factors'' \citep{powell2023}, and in March 2024 that ``there are many different financial conditions indicators, and you can kind of, you know, see different answers to that question'' \citep{powell2024}. A reading of financial conditions is a reading of policy only to the extent that a point of the index means a point of policy.

The two weightings answer different questions. The event study starts with a policy surprise and measures how asset prices move; it favors the assets that recover the surprise most precisely. The macro model starts with asset-price movements and measures how spending responds; it favors the assets with the largest effects on spending. There is no general reason for the two criteria to agree. Three distinct results describe how far they do. First, the market index reads one on the typical response to a policy surprise by construction. Second, the FRB/US weights price the same response at \bl{ag:h4:bpsi} funds-rate points (interval \bl{ag:h4:bpsi:lo} to \bl{ag:h4:bpsi:hi}), an empirical result that depends on the model experiment, a persistent shift in each variable. Third, dividing the FRB/US weights by that number puts the two readings on one scale and makes them agree exactly along the policy-news direction; the empirical check of agreement is then held-out announcements, where the difference between the readings is \bl{dz:ev:ratio} of the market move.

The readings differ on composition, and by about the same proportion at every horizon. For the same movements the model gives the dollar \bl{ag:h4:rr} times the relative effect that the event study does (interval \bl{ag:h4:rr:lo} to \bl{ag:h4:rr:hi}), and the contrast stays between \bl{ag:mp:hl8} and \bl{ag:mp:hl2} when the movements are assumed to decay with half-lives of 8 or 2 quarters. The difference between the two readings is about \bl{dl:k1:ann} of the market move on announcement days and \bl{dl:k1:oth} on other days, and about \bl{dl:lad22} at 22 days and \bl{dl:lad252} over a year (Table~\ref{tab:daily}, Figure~\ref{fig:D}). The earlier figure of \bl{dz:ev:ratio} for intraday announcement windows reflects windows in which the weights are fitted, not a property of announcements. History shows when the difference matters. In 1998--2001 the full FRB/US index averaged \bl{br:ep1998:gap} points tighter than the market index, of which \bl{br:ep1998:eq} came from the equity weights. In 2022--24 the full indexes also differed in their average levels, by \bl{br:ep2022:gap} points, while the two weightings applied to the same three assets agreed almost exactly on the year-over-year tightening ($D=$ \bl{ce:2022:D}); both overpredicted the rise in unemployment (Table~\ref{tab:epmain}).

\begin{figure}[tp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.93\textwidth]{../replication/report/fig_D.pdf}
\caption{Two Readings of the Same Financial Movements}
\label{fig:D}
\begin{flushleft}\footnotesize\textit{Notes:} Monthly, 12-month changes of the equity valuation, the 10-year yield and the dollar relative to $r^*$ (the inputs of the market index). Top: the change in the market index and the FRB/US reading of the same changes, normalized so that a conventional policy surprise reads one. Bottom: the difference $D$, FRB/US less market, with dotted lines at $\pm0.25$. Shaded: 1998--2001, 2004--06 and 2022--24.\end{flushleft}
\end{figure}

Realized activity cannot say which reading is better. Conditional on the market index, the composition term has a coefficient of \bl{rs:du:main:gamma} for the change in unemployment a year ahead (interval \bl{rs:du:main:gamma:lo} to \bl{rs:du:main:gamma:hi}), and the coefficient that the model's weights imply differs from it by \bl{rs:du:main:gmb} (\bl{rs:du:main:gmb:lo} to \bl{rs:du:main:gmb:hi}); neither restriction is rejected. That is an outlook difference of \bl{cm:du:main:eff} percentage point (\bl{cm:du:main:efflo} to \bl{cm:du:main:effhi}) between the 5th and 95th percentile of composition. The estimate tells what the sample can establish and leaves the measurement result intact.

The approach has three features. First, I put the two weightings on one scale and report the model's reading in its own units before normalizing. Second, I measure the difference between the readings on announcements and ordinary movements at the same horizons, and I account for it by the weights, the persistence of the paths and the contributions that only the FRB/US index has. Finally, I ask whether activity can tell the weightings apart: whether composition predicts unemployment and GDP beyond the index change, whether equity valuation alone does as well, and what composition predicts about the forecasts and the forecast errors of professionals. A short section reports what both indexes and the Chicago Fed's NFCI say about unemployment; the rest of the earlier comparison of indexes, with its nominal, real and neutral-rate versions, is in the appendices.

\subsection{Connection to the Literature}
\textbf{Weights and their meaning.} Indexes of financial conditions differ mainly in the source of their weights \citep{hatzius2010}: effects on output from a model \citep{dudley2000,hatzius2018,ajello2023}, principal components \citep{brave2011}, or event-study regressions \citep{kuttner2001,gurkaynak2005,bernanke2005,nakamura2018,bauer2023}. The older literature on monetary conditions indexes recognized that the weights on asset prices depend on the shock and the dynamics, so that their policy meaning is not invariant \citep{eika1996}. I quantify that difficulty with two observed sets of U.S. weights and ask where it matters.

\textbf{The closest comparisons.} \citet{arnaut2024} estimate how an existing index responds to policy surprises, \citet{sack2023} relate FCI-G to policy-induced changes in the two-year yield, and \citet{ajello2023} translate movements into a growth impulse with model multipliers. \citet{bletzinger2026} build a U.S. macro-finance index in units comparable to a policy rate and compare it with the Goldman Sachs index and FCI-G, finding divergences driven by equities and the strongest tightening in 2022--24. I ask how a policy-news weighting and a macro weighting read the same movements. \citet{boehm2025} show that announcements also move equities and exchange rates along a dimension orthogonal to yield changes, so a movement that departs from the conventional pattern can come from the announcement itself, which the event-study weights treat as noise.

\textbf{Information about activity.} \citet{hatzius2010} build an index conditional on past activity and inflation, \citet{aramonte2013} show that comparisons among indexes depend on the crisis in the sample, and \citet{lombardi2025} separate safe yields from priced risk and find that risk predicts activity more strongly. \citet{adrian2019} show that conditions shift the lower quantiles of growth, and \citet{brave2011} build an index of risk, credit and leverage. \citet{caballero2026} define FCI$^*$, the level of financial conditions consistent with output at potential, and the neutral rate matters for the level of every index \citep{laubach2003,holston2017,holston2023,hamilton2016,bauer2020,kiley2020}; I measure the inputs relative to $\rs$ in the appendices and do not estimate a neutral level for the index.
\FloatBarrier
"""

SEC2_ADD = r"""
\subsection{What Agreement Requires}\label{sec:requires}
Let $\psi$ be the responses of the three variables to a unit of policy news, $w_M$ the market weights (so that $w_M'\psi=1$), and $b_h$ the FRB/US weights for GDP at horizon $h$ in funds-rate points. The model prices the typical response at $b_h'\psi$, which I report in its own units first, and the normalized weights $w_{B,h}=b_h/(b_h'\psi)$ put both indexes on one scale. For a vector of financial movements $x$ the difference between the readings is
\[
D_h(x)=(w_{B,h}-w_M)'x.
\]
By construction $D_h(\psi s)=0$ for any conventional surprise $s$. Throughout, $D$ means this difference computed on the movements of the three variables over the horizon in question; in the regressions of Section~\ref{sec:outlook} it is computed on 12-month changes and is not differenced again. Agreement on other movements requires the difference in weights to have little exposure to the combinations of asset prices that occur. The weights can differ appreciably and give almost the same reading in practice, if the movements that occur resemble policy news, and two highly correlated indexes can disagree during a sustained equity revaluation. Table~\ref{tab:example} gives the arithmetic for two movements with the same market reading.

\input{../replication/report/tab_example}

The comparison confounds composition with persistence. A persistent dollar appreciation in FRB/US and the dollar's response to policy news are different experiments, so a difference in weights could reflect the initial composition of the movement or the assumed path that follows. Section~\ref{sec:persist} separates them. Appendix~\ref{app:ma} states the static argument.
\FloatBarrier
"""

SEC3 = r"""
% ============================================================
\section{When Do the Two Readings Agree?}\label{sec:agree}
\subsection{On Announcements}\label{sec:onannounce}
The FRB/US weights price the response of the three variables to a unit of policy news at \bl{ag:h2:bpsi}, \bl{ag:h4:bpsi} and \bl{ag:h8:bpsi} funds-rate points at two, four and eight quarters, with an interval of \bl{ag:h4:bpsi:lo} to \bl{ag:h4:bpsi:hi} at four quarters (Table~\ref{tab:agree}). The market index prices it at one, so the two do not differ significantly at any horizon. The agreement is specific to persistent paths. On the 253 announcements, with the weights re-estimated without the event being evaluated, the difference between the two readings has a mean absolute size of \bl{dz:ev:mean} against a mean absolute market move of \bl{dz:ev:move}, a ratio of \bl{dz:ev:ratio} (interval \bl{dz:ev:ratio:lo} to \bl{dz:ev:ratio:hi}), and the 90th percentile is \bl{dz:ev:p90}. These are intraday windows, in which the weights are fitted to the same events.

\input{../replication/report/tab_agree}

\subsection{At Other Horizons and on Other Days}\label{sec:ordinary}
The ratio of \bl{dz:ev:ratio} does not generalize to other days. On daily changes of the same three assets, 1996--2026, the difference is \bl{dl:k1:ann} of the market move on announcement days and \bl{dl:k1:oth} on other days; at one week it is \bl{dl:k5:ann} and \bl{dl:k5:oth}, and at 22 trading days \bl{dl:k22:ann} and \bl{dl:k22:oth} (Table~\ref{tab:daily}). Every difference between announcement and other windows has an interval that includes zero. Across horizons the ratio on days without announcements is \bl{dl:lad1}, \bl{dl:lad5}, \bl{dl:lad22}, \bl{dl:lad66} and \bl{dl:lad252} at 1, 5, 22, 66 and 252 days. Monthly and 12-month changes give \bl{dz:1m:ratio} and \bl{dz:12m:ratio} (Table~\ref{tab:dsize}). The difference is about a third of the move whatever the horizon, and the daily comparison does not show that the asset mix outside announcements is the source of the disagreement. In 12-month changes the difference exceeds 0.25 in \bl{dz:12m:sh25} percent of months (interval \bl{dz:12m:sh25:lo} to \bl{dz:12m:sh25:hi}) and 0.5 in \bl{dz:12m:sh50} percent (Figure~\ref{fig:D}); the months overlap, so the runs in this series are not independent evidence of persistence. A 12-month window always contains announcements, and the daily comparison uses assets measured at the nominal step.

\input{../replication/report/tab_daily}
\input{../replication/report/tab_dsize}

\subsection{Composition or Persistence?}\label{sec:persist}
The FRB/US dollar-to-equity ratio is \bl{ag:h2:fr}, \bl{ag:h4:fr} and \bl{ag:h8:fr} at two, four and eight quarters, against \bl{ag:mkt:ratio} for the market weights (interval \bl{ag:mkt:ratio:lo} to \bl{ag:mkt:ratio:hi}). The ratio of ratios at four quarters is \bl{ag:h4:rr} (\bl{ag:h4:rr:lo} to \bl{ag:h4:rr:hi}); at two quarters its interval includes one. The contrast does not come from the assumed persistence. Pricing the same movements with a common decaying path, with half-lives of 8, 4 and 2 quarters, gives dollar-to-equity ratios of \bl{ag:mp:hl8}, \bl{ag:mp:hl4} and \bl{ag:mp:hl2} at four quarters, against \bl{ag:h4:fr} for the persistent paths, and different half-lives for the two assets move the ratio between \bl{ag:mp:min} and \bl{ag:mp:max}, never near one. Decay changes the scale of the typical response, not the contrast: the model's reading of the typical response falls to \bl{ag:bp:hl4} (\bl{ag:bp:hl4:lo} to \bl{ag:bp:hl4:hi}) with a half-life of four quarters. The data give no support for short half-lives (the half-lives of the levels of the three variables since 1996 are 20 to 26 quarters, and the equity response to policy news does not decay within 20 trading days). The contrast is a difference in the effects on GDP per unit of each asset.

\subsection{When the Weights Would Have Changed the Reading}\label{sec:accounting}
Table~\ref{tab:epmain} sets three episodes side by side. The left block is the difference in the average levels of the full FRB/US index and the market index, built as a bridge of stages on the same inputs and centering (Table~\ref{tab:bridge}); the middle block is the difference $D$ in 12-month changes on the three matched assets; the right block is what followed. They are different objects: a gap in levels can coexist with agreement in changes.

\input{../replication/report/tab_epmain}

In 1998--2001 the FRB/US index read \bl{br:ep1998:gap} points tighter than the market index (interval \bl{br:ep1998:lo} to \bl{br:ep1998:hi}), of which the equity weights contribute \bl{br:ep1998:eq}; the market index puts more weight on valuations that were far above average. $D$ averaged \bl{ce:1998:D}, and unemployment rose by \bl{ce:1998:real} against forecasts of \bl{ce:1998:fcm} from the market index and \bl{ce:1998:fcf} from the FRB/US weights: the choice of weights would have changed the reading, and the outcome does not say which was right. In 2022--24 the full indexes differ by \bl{br:ep2022:gap} (\bl{br:ep2022:lo} to \bl{br:ep2022:hi}) in levels, with equities (\bl{br:ep2022:eq}) and the dollar (\bl{br:ep2022:usd}) contributing about equally, but $D$ is \bl{ce:2022:D}: on the same three assets the two weightings agree on the year-over-year tightening of \bl{ce:2022:dm}. Unemployment rose by \bl{ce:2022:real}, against forecasts of \bl{ce:2022:fcm} (market) and \bl{ce:2022:fcf} (FRB/US weights). Changing the weights would have done little to explain the miss. In 2004--06 the gap in levels is \bl{br:ep2004:gap} (\bl{br:ep2004:lo} to \bl{br:ep2004:hi}): the equity weights add \bl{br:ep2004:eq} and house prices subtract \bl{abs:br:ep2004:house}, so equities do not explain it.

\input{../replication/report/tab_bridge}
\FloatBarrier

% ============================================================
\section{Can Realized Activity Tell the Weightings Apart?}\label{sec:outlook}
\subsection{One Test}\label{sec:onetest}
Conditional on the market index, does composition contain information about unemployment a year ahead? I regress the 12-month-ahead change in the unemployment gap on own lags and $y=\cdots+\beta M+\gamma D$, where $M$ is the 12-month change in the market index (relative to $r^*$) and $D$ the 12-month change of the normalized FRB/US-minus-market difference on the same changes. If the market reading is right, $\gamma=0$. If the normalized FRB/US reading $M+D$ is right, $\gamma=\beta$. Four-quarter GDP growth is the model-facing check, since GDP is the outcome that constructs the FRB/US weights. This is the only primary test; Table~\ref{tab:comp} reports both restrictions with the joint covariance of the estimates.

\subsection{The Answer}\label{sec:answer}
On 1976--2026 the coefficient on the index change is \bl{rs:du:main:beta} and on composition \bl{rs:du:main:gamma} (\bl{rs:du:main:gamma:lo} to \bl{rs:du:main:gamma:hi}); the difference $\gamma-\beta$ is \bl{rs:du:main:gmb} (\bl{rs:du:main:gmb:lo} to \bl{rs:du:main:gmb:hi}). Neither restriction is rejected. Moving $D$ from its 5th to its 95th percentile at the same index change shifts the year-ahead outlook by \bl{cm:du:main:eff} percentage point, with an interval from \bl{cm:du:main:efflo} to \bl{cm:du:main:effhi}. On 1996--2026 the coefficient on composition is \bl{rs:du:1996p:gamma} (\bl{rs:du:1996p:gamma:lo} to \bl{rs:du:1996p:gamma:hi}) and the model's restriction is rejected ($\gamma-\beta=$ \bl{rs:du:1996p:gmb}, \bl{rs:du:1996p:gmb:lo} to \bl{rs:du:1996p:gmb:hi}); excluding 2008--15 the coefficients are \bl{rs:du:ex200815:gamma} and \bl{rs:du:ex200815:gmb}, close to the model's restriction. The sign changes across samples. The market restriction is not rejected in any of \bl{rs:ntests} blocks, and the model's restriction is rejected in \bl{rs:nrej:gmb} of them, without correction for the number of tests. For GDP growth neither restriction is rejected. In no sample does adding $D$ significantly improve out-of-sample forecasts (Clark--West \bl{cm:addD:cw} on 1976--2026). The data do not choose between the two readings in the main sample; where they choose, they reject the model's.

\input{../replication/report/tab_comp}

\subsection{Equities Alone}\label{sec:eqalone}
Equity valuation alone raises the adjusted $R^2$ of the change in unemployment by \bl{cm:eq:gain}, the market index by \bl{cm:mkt:gain}, the FRB/US weights by \bl{cm:frbus:gain}, the 10-year yield by \bl{cm:y10:gain}, the dollar by \bl{cm:usd:gain} and the three variables entered freely by \bl{cm:free:gain}. Out of sample, equity valuation alone forecasts better than the market index (Diebold--Mariano \bl{cm:eqmkt:dm}, an error ratio of \bl{cm:eqmkt:rmse}) and the FRB/US weights worse (\bl{cm:frmkt:dm}), without correction for the number of tests. Entered freely, the coefficients are not significantly different from proportional to the market weights ($p=$ \bl{cm:prop:wm}) or to the FRB/US weights ($p=$ \bl{cm:prop:wb}); the power is low. Equity valuation carries most of the content, which is why the data have difficulty ranking the weightings: the movements that distinguish them are the ones in which the dollar and the equity valuation move in different proportions.

\subsection{What Forecasters Expected and What Happened}\label{sec:spf}
Forecasters' errors combine the outcome with what they had already anticipated, so they do not test the FRB/US multipliers directly. Table~\ref{tab:spf} regresses the Survey of Professional Forecasters' unemployment forecast, the realized change and the error, over the same \bl{cm:spf:n} surveys (1990Q3--2025Q3), on the index change and $D$. When FRB/US reads the same asset movements as tighter than the market index does, unemployment tends to come in below the survey forecast and GDP growth above it. The coefficient on $D$ is \bl{sd:u:all:error} for the error (\bl{sd:u:all:error:lo} to \bl{sd:u:all:error:hi}) and \bl{sd:u:all:realized} for the realization (\bl{sd:u:all:realized:lo} to \bl{sd:u:all:realized:hi}), against \bl{sd:u:all:forecast} for the forecast (\bl{sd:u:all:forecast:lo} to \bl{sd:u:all:forecast:hi}): composition predicts what subsequently happened, not what forecasters expected. Excluding 2008--15 the error coefficient is \bl{sd:u:ex200815:error} (\bl{sd:u:ex200815:error:lo} to \bl{sd:u:ex200815:error:hi}). The sign is the opposite of what the model's reading implies if forecasters had not priced the composition. The result is suggestive only: the outcomes overlap, the coefficients are not adjusted for their number, and 2001 and 2008--09 probably dominate.

\input{../replication/report/tab_spf}
\FloatBarrier

% ============================================================
\section{What the Two Indexes Have in Common}\label{sec:prices}
The two weightings are hard to tell apart with outcomes partly because both carry the same information about unemployment. Both predict unemployment a year ahead through their 12-month changes, not their levels: the changes have $t$-statistics of \bl{lf:du:mkt:t:flw:both} (market index), \bl{lf:du:gs:t:flw:both} (the Goldman Sachs replication) and \bl{lf:du:frbus:t:flw:both} (FRB/US index) and the levels \bl{lf:du:mkt:t:lvl:both}, \bl{lf:du:gs:t:lvl:both} and \bl{lf:du:frbus:t:lvl:both} (Appendix~\ref{app:levelflow}). The three add \bl{st:du:flow:gain}, \bl{st:du:gs:gain} and \bl{st:du:frbus:gain} to the adjusted $R^2$ beyond the real funds rate relative to $\rs$, so this is evidence about financial prices and not about the weighting (Appendix~\ref{app:stance}). The Chicago Fed's NFCI is a different index, a level of credit, risk and leverage: beside the market flow it carries the longer horizons and recession risk, while both keep their coefficients for unemployment at 6 and 12 months ($t=$ \bl{nf:du6:mkt:t} and \bl{nf:du12:mkt:t} for the market flow, \bl{nf:du6:nfci:t} and \bl{nf:du12:nfci:t} for the NFCI). Purging the inputs of what lagged unemployment and inflation explain cuts the response of unemployment to the market index by \bl{pg:fall:full} to \bl{pg:fall:rec} percent and leaves it above zero; the flow carries both a channel and an anticipation component. The results hold on 1996--2026 with smaller gains and not for payrolls, and real-time $\rs$ weakens them (Appendix~\ref{app:robust}). Measuring the inputs relative to the neutral rate changes the readings, and outcomes lean toward the $\rs$ basis without proving it (Appendix~\ref{app:steps}).
\FloatBarrier
"""

DISCUSSION = r"""
% ============================================================
\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:discussion}
The paper establishes how to read a movement in financial conditions as a movement in policy under two calibrations, and where the readings part. For the typical response to a policy surprise the two readings coincide: by normalization for the market index, and for FRB/US at \bl{ag:h4:bpsi} when movements persist. They part on composition. The model gives the dollar about \bl{ag:h4:rr} times the relative effect that the event study does, assumed persistence does not change that, and the difference between the readings is about a third of the market move at every horizon. Whether a given tightening would have been read differently depends on its source. In 1998--2001 the choice of weights moved the reading by \bl{br:ep1998:gap} points, mostly through equities. In 2022--24 the two weightings agreed on the year's tightening although the full indexes differed in their average levels, and neither explains the miss in unemployment.

What realized activity can add is limited. Conditional on the market index, composition does not predict unemployment or GDP with a sign or size that the sample can pin down, the two restrictions are not rejected in the main sample, and equity valuation alone carries most of the content. Composition predicts what forecasters got wrong, not what they expected, with the sign opposite to the model's reading; that is suggestive and not decisive. A reader who wants to use either index can say what a movement is equivalent to in policy under each, and can expect the two to differ when equities and the dollar move in different proportions. That is a statement about measurement. The causal effect of the movement on spending, and its source, remain separate questions; a movement that departs from the policy-news mix can come from the announcement itself \citep{boehm2025}.

The limits are those of the design. The FRB/US weights come from one model and one experiment and are held fixed in the intervals; the market weights are imprecise, the 10-year weight most of all; the horizons are compared on overlapping data; the daily comparison uses nominal assets; and there are five recessions in the sample, two of them out of sample. There is no ground truth for financial conditions, and zero is the 1973--2026 average of each variable, not a neutral level. The most durable finding is the contrast in composition, its proportion at every horizon, and the two episodes that show when it matters; the answer to whether it matters for activity is open.
\FloatBarrier
"""

APPA = r"""
\section{The Market Index and the FRB/US Index: a Static Argument}\label{app:ma}
Let financial movements be $x=\psi s+u$, where $s$ is policy news and $u$ collects everything else with covariance $\Sigma$, and let $g_h$ be the effects of a persistent unit shift in each variable on spending at horizon $h$. The FRB/US weights are proportional to $g_h$, and the market weights are $w_M\propto\Sigma^{-1}\psi$ (generalized least squares, normalized so that $w_M'\psi=1$). For any $x$ the difference between the normalized readings is $D_h(x)=(w_{B,h}-w_M)'x=(w_{B,h}-w_M)'u$, because the policy part cancels. The two readings coincide for every movement if and only if $w_{B,h}=w_M$, that is, if $g_h\propto\Sigma^{-1}\psi$: the criterion that recovers policy news precisely and the criterion that measures spending effects select the same weights only in that case. If the observed movements lie along one direction, $\psi$, then every weight vector normalized to price $\psi$ at one gives the same reading on them, and the data identify the reading along that direction while leaving the individual weights undetermined. What distinguishes the weightings is the movements off that direction, and the variance of the difference is $(w_{B,h}-w_M)'\Sigma(w_{B,h}-w_M)$, which Table~\ref{tab:dsize} estimates as a ratio to the market move.
"""
